indicative of this white oval for user input.

00:32:48.021 --> 00:32:50.732
If I want to prompt the user with that same phrase,

00:32:50.942 --> 00:32:51.771
what's your name?

00:32:51.942 --> 00:32:51.982
Well,

00:32:52.062 --> 00:32:54.972
I can just put it inside of those parentheses.

00:32:55.182 --> 00:32:56.832
But what next do I need to add

00:32:57.182 --> 00:32:58.302
around my user input?

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:01.160
Yeah,

00:33:01.199 --> 00:33:03.589
I need the quotation marks just to make clear

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:06.089
that these aren't special individual words.

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:09.670
This is a whole phrase that I want to be displayed to the user,

00:33:09.800 --> 00:33:12.709
so I'm going to indeed put double quotes around everything,

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:14.069
and this is just an aesthetic.

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:17.400
I don't in this case want to bother moving the cursor to the next line.

00:33:17.584 --> 00:33:20.015
Like I want the user to see the question

00:33:20.145 --> 00:33:22.224
and I want the cursor to just stay there blinking,

00:33:22.425 --> 00:33:23.454
waiting for their prompt,

00:33:23.505 --> 00:33:25.994
but I don't want the cursor to be right next to the question mark.

00:33:26.185 --> 00:33:29.305
So I'm deliberately just leaving a single white space there just to kind

00:33:29.305 --> 00:33:31.574
of scooch it over a bit so it looks a little prettier,

00:33:31.744 --> 00:33:32.775
at least to my eye.

00:33:32.905 --> 00:33:33.905
Now we're not done yet.

00:33:34.319 --> 00:33:37.130
Because we need to do something with this value.

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:38.170
The get string function,

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:38.890
as we'll soon see,

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:41.839
is going to prompt the user for me to type something in like my name,

00:33:42.089 --> 00:33:43.569
but where do I want to put that?

00:33:43.689 --> 00:33:43.770
Well,

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:47.729
MIT has the answer put in a variable called answer,

00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:49.239
and you can't rename that in scratch.

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:51.599
It's just defined as answer.

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:52.319
But

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:53.239
in C,

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.800
what I'm going to need to do is something like this.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:34:00.160
If you want to keep return values around from a function,

00:34:00.369 --> 00:34:02.130
you literally use an equal sign.

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:08.050
And then to the left of it you put the name of the variable into which you want to put

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:09.550
that return value.

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:11.188
So in mathematics we would use X,

00:34:11.199 --> 00:34:11.300
Y,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:12.399
and Z as our variables.

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:13.330
Again in code,

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:14.260
as in scratch,

00:34:14.458 --> 00:34:16.379
you can name your variables anything you want.

00:34:16.489 --> 00:34:18.300
By convention they should usually be lowercase.

00:34:18.379 --> 00:34:19.938
They should not have spaces therein,

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:21.219
similar to file names,

00:34:21.379 --> 00:34:24.620
but this is a pretty good analog now of what's going on collectively.

00:34:24.864 --> 00:34:25.495
Here,

00:34:25.705 --> 00:34:27.254
but C is a little more

00:34:27.864 --> 00:34:28.614
precise.

00:34:29.145 --> 00:34:31.094
You can't just give the variable a name.

00:34:31.385 --> 00:34:35.014
You need to tell C or really the compiler,

00:34:35.344 --> 00:34:38.665
what type of value you want to put in this variable.

00:34:38.705 --> 00:34:39.853
So if it's a string of text,

00:34:40.024 --> 00:34:41.054
you put string.

00:34:41.103 --> 00:34:41.813
If it's a number,

00:34:41.824 --> 00:34:42.813
you're going to put something else.

00:34:42.905 --> 00:34:44.135
But for now it's a string

00:34:44.645 --> 00:34:46.594
per the function's name it's going to give me

00:34:46.985 --> 00:34:48.014
a string.

00:34:48.425 --> 00:34:50.264
Now we're so close to finishing this comparison.

00:34:50.344 --> 00:34:51.824
There's one detail missing.

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:54.478
What's still missing from the code here?

00:34:55.388 --> 00:34:55.398
Yeah,

00:34:57.168 --> 00:34:57.178
yeah,

00:34:57.299 --> 00:35:00.448
so we have to finish the thought lastly with a semicolon.

00:35:00.499 --> 00:35:02.118
So if you're getting the sort of the point already,

00:35:02.178 --> 00:35:04.178
like this is one of the reasons why we start with scratch,

00:35:04.218 --> 00:35:06.739
you sort of you get the intuition pretty quickly and even

00:35:06.739 --> 00:35:08.658
though nothing on the right hand side is particularly hard,

00:35:08.739 --> 00:35:10.339
there's just all these stupid little details that

00:35:10.339 --> 00:35:12.099
you have to ingrain in yourself over time.

00:35:12.409 --> 00:35:13.070
In this case,

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:13.550
for C,

00:35:13.629 --> 00:35:16.780
but for many programming languages we're going to see the similar paradigm.

00:35:16.989 --> 00:35:18.629
But among the goals of the course too are

00:35:18.629 --> 00:35:21.030
to show you how ultimately languages have been evolving.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:23.669
And so one of the things we'll see in Python in a few weeks' time

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:28.229
that some of this syntax actually goes away because over time humans.

00:35:28.314 --> 00:35:30.145
Gotten annoyed at older languages like this.

00:35:30.304 --> 00:35:32.145
Like why the heck do I have to keep putting a semicolon

00:35:32.145 --> 00:35:33.895
when it's clear that I'm at the end of the line?

00:35:34.104 --> 00:35:36.784
So we'll see among languages like Python we can

00:35:36.784 --> 00:35:38.895
get rid of some of these same features.

00:35:38.945 --> 00:35:42.455
But for now it's just a matter of remembering what goes where.

