02:19:44.859 --> 02:19:45.689
in memory.

02:19:45.818 --> 02:19:46.189
In fact,

02:19:46.338 --> 02:19:48.018
integer overflow is a term of art.

02:19:48.156 --> 02:19:53.666
Whereby you can overflow an integer by trying to store too big of a value in it,

02:19:53.675 --> 02:19:55.656
and the reason for this is again to make this clear,

02:19:55.826 --> 02:19:59.975
this is a piece of memory inside of a laptop or a desktop or some other device,

02:20:00.185 --> 02:20:02.505
and in these little black chips is a whole bunch

02:20:02.505 --> 02:20:05.906
of bits or really bytes that can store information electronically,

02:20:05.946 --> 02:20:08.196
but they allocate those bits in units of 8,

02:20:08.385 --> 02:20:08.996
maybe 16,

02:20:09.146 --> 02:20:09.786
maybe 32,

02:20:09.896 --> 02:20:10.646
maybe 64,

02:20:10.666 --> 02:20:13.055
but finitely many per value.

02:20:13.242 --> 02:20:15.442
Whether we're using 32 or 64,

02:20:15.572 --> 02:20:18.551
you can only count so high if you have a finite number of bits,

02:20:18.631 --> 02:20:21.902
and we've seen this problem even on a small scale with our flat light bulbs last week.

02:20:22.032 --> 02:20:25.032
If we have a 3 digit number as represented by like

02:20:25.032 --> 02:20:28.141
3 physical light bulbs or 3 tiny transistors in the computer,

02:20:28.311 --> 02:20:34.541
I can count from 0 to 1 to 2 to 3 to 4 to 5 to 6 to 7.

02:20:34.751 --> 02:20:36.421
If I want to count to 8 though,

02:20:36.711 --> 02:20:38.171
I need a 4th bit.

02:20:38.618 --> 02:20:40.227
But as the red suggests,

02:20:40.278 --> 02:20:41.717
if you don't have a 4th bit,

02:20:41.917 --> 02:20:43.257
for all intents and purposes,

02:20:43.398 --> 02:20:45.408
that number is just 0.

02:20:45.677 --> 02:20:46.597
Or as an aside,

02:20:46.797 --> 02:20:48.398
