02:22:14.860 --> 02:22:17.889
So picture here is a Boeing 787 from a few years back,

02:22:18.009 --> 02:22:19.889
long before there were all the more recent problems with.

02:22:20.309 --> 02:22:22.360
planes whereby after

02:22:22.790 --> 02:22:26.309
248 days of continuous power,

02:22:26.469 --> 02:22:29.299
which is kind of a thing in the aviation industry like time is money,

02:22:29.429 --> 02:22:31.769
and generally they want the planes in the air as much as possible,

02:22:31.830 --> 02:22:33.900
which means they want them powered on as much as possible,

02:22:34.030 --> 02:22:35.589
which means they don't like turn them off at night.

02:22:35.669 --> 02:22:37.219
They keep them going and flying.

02:22:37.469 --> 02:22:39.379
After 248 days,

02:22:39.509 --> 02:22:43.110
the New York Times reported a few years back that a Model 787 airplane.

02:22:43.660 --> 02:22:48.820
That has been powered continuously for 248 days can lose all alternating current

02:22:48.820 --> 02:22:50.860
electrical power due to the generator control

02:22:50.860 --> 02:22:53.049
unit simultaneously going into fail-safe mode.

02:22:53.280 --> 02:22:55.830
This condition is caused by a software counter internal to the

02:22:55.830 --> 02:22:59.290
GCUs that will overflow after 248 days of continuous power.

02:22:59.540 --> 02:23:03.259
Boeing is in the process at the time of developing a GCU software upgrade.

02:23:03.360 --> 02:23:03.980
Will remedy

02:23:04.150 --> 02:23:05.259
the unsafe condition.

02:23:05.429 --> 02:23:09.429
So literally what this means is that the power to these planes would just shut

02:23:09.429 --> 02:23:13.519
off if the planes were on for more than 248 days at a time,

02:23:13.549 --> 02:23:17.339
and this was a common thing for lanes to be maximal power.

02:23:17.870 --> 02:23:20.469
Why was this actually happening or what was the solution?

02:23:20.549 --> 02:23:20.589
Well,

02:23:20.629 --> 02:23:21.429
the short term fix,

02:23:21.509 --> 02:23:23.219
because it took a while for Boeing to fix this,

02:23:23.750 --> 02:23:24.549
was what?

02:23:24.669 --> 02:23:27.179
What would you do if the symptom is that the plane

02:23:27.709 --> 02:23:30.219
shuts off midflight after 248 days,

02:23:30.309 --> 02:23:30.780
yeah.

02:23:32.419 --> 02:23:34.820
Literally turn it off and back on again,

02:23:34.980 --> 02:23:37.559
much like you've probably been taught with your phones and computers

02:23:37.559 --> 02:23:40.570
and any other electronic devices that somehow freak out on occasion.

02:23:40.820 --> 02:23:41.620
Reboot the plane.

02:23:41.740 --> 02:23:42.580
Now why is that?

02:23:42.700 --> 02:23:42.740
Well,

02:23:42.820 --> 02:23:45.900
any time you reboot a phone or a laptop or a plane,

02:23:46.219 --> 02:23:49.620
all of those variables get reset to their default values,

02:23:49.700 --> 02:23:49.809
which,

02:23:49.820 --> 02:23:50.980
if it's the first line of code,

02:23:51.059 --> 02:23:52.059
like in some of my examples,

02:23:52.139 --> 02:23:53.900
get set back to zero again.

02:23:53.980 --> 02:23:54.620
For instance,

02:23:54.660 --> 02:23:57.530
the first line of code is executed from top to bottom.

02:23:57.759 --> 02:23:59.139
So this effectively solved the.

02:23:59.224 --> 02:23:59.455
Problem.

02:23:59.535 --> 02:24:00.974
But when they finally rolled out a fix,

02:24:01.054 --> 02:24:02.294
then you didn't have to do that anymore.

02:24:02.334 --> 02:24:04.655
But the source of the problem is essentially

02:24:04.655 --> 02:24:07.365
that they were probably using 32 bit integers,

02:24:07.375 --> 02:24:08.415
but also negative value,

02:24:08.495 --> 02:24:12.415
so they had 31 bits at their disposal to count to positive numbers,

02:24:12.445 --> 02:24:18.285
and 248 days is roughly how many tens of a second there are,

02:24:18.535 --> 02:24:22.565
which means once you count in tens of a second for 248 days

02:24:22.565 --> 02:24:26.254
you would overflow an integer and the power would shut off effectively because.

02:24:26.389 --> 02:24:28.309
Something ended up going to zero,

02:24:28.450 --> 02:24:29.719
so there was a lot of sort of

02:24:30.250 --> 02:24:31.730
marketing speak or technical speak in that,

02:24:31.769 --> 02:24:34.639
but it boiled down to just a simple integer overflow.

02:24:34.809 --> 02:24:37.290
There's a historical bug in Pac-Man if you've

02:24:37.290 --> 02:24:40.269
ever played this in any of its forms whereby

02:24:40.490 --> 02:24:43.240
you can play up to level 255,

02:24:43.330 --> 02:24:46.599
but because there was a missing if condition that checked what level you were on,

02:24:46.730 --> 02:24:50.400
you could accidentally garble the screen if you were amazing at Pac-Man

02:24:50.400 --> 02:24:53.410
because they too would overflow an integer and just random character.

02:24:53.655 --> 02:24:54.995
would end up appearing on the screen,

02:24:55.165 --> 02:24:57.125
so it's sort of like a badge of honor to actually

02:24:57.125 --> 02:25:00.674
hit level 256 in this way because of this bug.

