02:25:52.280 --> 02:25:54.559
missing a double quote there was an unintended bug.

02:25:54.639 --> 02:25:54.750
So,

02:25:54.759 --> 02:25:55.910
if I make this here calculator,

02:25:55.959 --> 02:25:57.110
do slash calculator,

02:25:57.200 --> 02:25:57.759
type in 1,

02:25:57.839 --> 02:25:58.589
type in 3.

02:25:59.000 --> 02:25:59.990
I get 0,

02:26:00.070 --> 02:26:01.019
which is weird.

02:26:01.269 --> 02:26:04.059
What if I do instead maybe 2 and 3?

02:26:04.269 --> 02:26:06.309
It's 0 instead of 0.66.

02:26:06.400 --> 02:26:07.740
What if I do 3 and 3?

02:26:07.830 --> 02:26:07.870
Well,

02:26:07.950 --> 02:26:09.179
that curiously works,

02:26:09.269 --> 02:26:10.910
but if I do something like 4 and 3,

02:26:10.950 --> 02:26:12.790
which would be 1.33,

02:26:12.950 --> 02:26:15.080
that 2 doesn't seem to work.

02:26:15.230 --> 02:26:17.110
So there's this other issue in computing when

02:26:17.110 --> 02:26:18.709
you have finite numbers of bits known as

02:26:18.709 --> 02:26:21.349
truncation whereby even when you're trying to do

02:26:21.349 --> 02:26:23.950
floating point math like with a decimal point,

02:26:24.309 --> 02:26:26.219
if you are using an integer,

02:26:26.379 --> 02:26:26.750
you're going to.

02:26:26.901 --> 02:26:30.250
Throw away everything after the decimal point unless

02:26:30.250 --> 02:26:32.600
you're explicitly using the right data type,

02:26:32.731 --> 02:26:34.680
and we saw an allusion to this earlier.

02:26:34.850 --> 02:26:38.970
If I actually go in now and change my values from integers to floats

02:26:38.970 --> 02:26:43.081
and change the percent I to a percent F and remake this calculator,

02:26:43.291 --> 02:26:47.310
now I can do 1 divided by 3 and I actually get back that their response.

02:26:47.571 --> 02:26:51.130
But there's another issue latent here which happens to in the real world

02:26:51.130 --> 02:26:54.090
whereby I'm going to tweak this percent F to be a little arcane.

02:26:54.250 --> 02:26:54.741
It turns out.

02:26:54.801 --> 02:26:59.072
You can see how many digits you want to show how many significant digits you want,

02:26:59.122 --> 02:26:59.662
if you will,

02:26:59.952 --> 02:27:03.181
by just using a dot and then a number like 50 arbitrarily.

02:27:03.391 --> 02:27:05.551
And contrary to what you might have learned in grade school,

02:27:05.652 --> 02:27:07.421
this calculator would seem to think

02:27:07.751 --> 02:27:10.942
that calculator 1 divided by 3

02:27:11.272 --> 02:27:15.181
is not 0.33333333 infinitely many times.

02:27:15.311 --> 02:27:18.022
There's all this random stuff happening at the end.

02:27:18.311 --> 02:27:19.112
Long story short,

02:27:19.192 --> 02:27:22.591
this is because computers one only use finitely many bits even.

02:27:22.703 --> 02:27:25.613
To represent floating point numbers and if there's an infinite number of those,

02:27:25.733 --> 02:27:28.962
you can't possibly represent every possible floating point value.

02:27:29.092 --> 02:27:32.792
So we're essentially seeing an approximation of 1/3 precisely,

02:27:32.892 --> 02:27:35.323
but this too happens quite a bit in the wild.

02:27:35.533 --> 02:27:38.212
There's really no solution to this other than by throwing more

02:27:38.212 --> 02:27:41.323
bits at the problem using a double instead of a flow,

02:27:41.533 --> 02:27:44.623
or at least somehow trying to detect this and catch this.

02:27:44.852 --> 02:27:47.403
That then is what we'd call floating point imprecision.

02:27:47.453 --> 02:27:50.533
But to tie this together and sort of induce a bit of fear and for the coming years.

02:27:50.954 --> 02:27:52.513
These things happen all of the time.

02:27:52.553 --> 02:27:53.683
Back when I was finishing school,

02:27:53.753 --> 02:27:57.574
there was the so-called Y2K problem or year 2000 problem whereby

02:27:57.793 --> 02:28:00.994
for decades computers had been using not 4 digits to

02:28:00.994 --> 02:28:03.463
represent years but just two because it was convenient,

02:28:03.713 --> 02:28:06.353
it was more efficient because you use half as much memory to

02:28:06.353 --> 02:28:09.944
represent maybe the year 1999 just using two digits instead of 4.

02:28:10.154 --> 02:28:15.343
Of course when the year rolled around from 1999 to 2000,

02:28:15.353 --> 02:28:17.574
if you didn't have these numbers even in memory,

02:28:17.753 --> 02:28:18.343
you might confuse.

02:28:18.894 --> 02:28:21.405
2000 with 1900,

02:28:21.495 --> 02:28:23.964
which was the presumption if you're only storing two digits.

02:28:24.075 --> 02:28:26.134
So we screwed that up and thankfully the world scrambled,

02:28:26.144 --> 02:28:28.214
and if you read up on Wikipedia and news articles from

02:28:28.214 --> 02:28:30.464
the time everyone thought the world might very well end,

02:28:30.884 --> 02:28:31.644
but it didn't,

02:28:31.775 --> 02:28:33.485
so you'd think we'd learned our lesson.

02:28:33.695 --> 02:28:34.214
Unfortunately,

02:28:34.334 --> 02:28:37.325
another such problem is coming up in the year 2038,

02:28:37.415 --> 02:28:40.724
whereby historically since the 70s and prior,

02:28:41.214 --> 02:28:44.905
computers have generally used 32 bit integers to keep track of time,

02:28:45.044 --> 02:28:46.254
the date and the time.

02:28:46.406 --> 02:28:50.066
By means of counting how many seconds have passed since January 1st,

02:28:50.236 --> 02:28:51.585
1970,

02:28:51.595 --> 02:28:53.795
and all of the math is just relative to that date because

02:28:53.795 --> 02:28:56.396
that's when computers were really starting to come onto the scene,

02:28:56.475 --> 02:28:57.026
if you will.

02:28:57.225 --> 02:28:57.835
Unfortunately,

02:28:57.916 --> 02:29:00.835
there's only 4 billion values you can count to or

02:29:00.835 --> 02:29:03.036
2 billion if you're doing negatives from January 1st,

02:29:03.625 --> 02:29:05.225
1970.

02:29:05.236 --> 02:29:05.946
And so,

02:29:06.396 --> 02:29:06.576
um,

02:29:06.616 --> 02:29:08.446
on the date January 19th,

02:29:08.515 --> 02:29:09.706
2038,

02:29:10.036 --> 02:29:14.196
we will overflow a 32 bit counter and suddenly if this problem is not.

02:29:14.306 --> 02:29:17.466
Fixed by you or other people before the year 2038,

02:29:17.617 --> 02:29:21.926
our computers and phones and other devices may very well think it's December 13,

02:29:22.296 --> 02:29:23.976
1901.

02:29:24.457 --> 02:29:24.926
So

02:29:25.216 --> 02:29:26.487
there are solutions to these problems.

02:29:26.626 --> 02:29:29.256
CS 50 is all about empowering you with solutions to these problems,

02:29:29.336 --> 02:29:31.126
but if you'd like to scan this here code,

02:29:31.527 --> 02:29:31.537
um,

02:29:31.577 --> 02:29:35.527
this will add that date to your Google calendar or your Outlook calendar.

02:29:35.777 --> 02:29:36.686
Keep an eye on it.

02:29:36.897 --> 02:29:38.747
That though is week one for CS 50.

02:29:38.777 --> 02:29:40.447
Problem set one will be in your hands soon.

02:29:40.617 --> 02:29:42.046
We'll see you next time.

